 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels and the number of slices [S1, S2, unofficial]. Performance can be improved by optimization techniques like empty space skipping and occlusion clipping [S2, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the volume size, limited by available texture memory [S1, S3, unofficial].
--   **Measured Performance:** Li et al. [40] reported an average of 2.8 speedup using optimization techniques like occlusion clipping in fragment programs [S2, unofficial].
--   **Scaling:** Performance degrades if the volume exceeds texture memory, requiring swapping [S1, unofficial]. Scaling to higher resolutions is limited by texture memory and fill rate [S2, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Hardware-accelerated interpolation, simple implementation, good image quality with trilinear interpolation [S3, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** Cannot skip unseen voxels, limited by texture memory size, requires careful filter selection [S1, S2, unofficial].
--   **When to Use:** Suitable for moderate-sized volumes that fit in texture memory, when hardware interpolation is preferred over manual ray marching [S2, S3, unofficial].
--   **When Not to Use:** For very large volumes that exceed texture memory, or when skipping unseen voxels is critical for performance [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Time Complexity:** Not explicitly stated in the sources, but performance scales with pixel count and slice count, with optimizations like occlusion clipping yielding a 2.8× speedup [S2, unofficial].
+-   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to volume size, constrained by available texture memory [S1, S3, unofficial].
+-   **Measured Performance:** Li et al. reported an average of 2.8 speedup using optimization techniques like occlusion clipping in fragment programs [S2, unofficial].
+-   **Scaling:** Scaling to higher resolutions is limited by texture memory, requiring paging or out-of-core techniques for large datasets [S2, unofficial].
+-   **Strengths:** Hardware-accelerated trilinear interpolation eliminates bands from 2D texturization and supports arbitrary viewing orientations [S3, S5, unofficial].
+-   **Limitations:** Cannot skip unseen voxels, limited by texture memory size, and projection-oriented polygons may introduce horizontal or vertical bands depending on polygon count [S1, S5, unofficial].
+-   **When to Use:** Moderate-sized volumes fitting in texture memory, when hardware interpolation is preferred [S2, S3, unofficial].
+-   **When Not to Use:** Volumes exceeding texture memory capacity, or when skipping unseen voxels is critical for performance [S1, unofficial].
 
